[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports hold three
problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one. Each problem now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The #4471 page already shows this. Its cause table marks <code>hint</code> as "no ticket of its
own", and a footnote under <code>parser</code> counts one-day moves. Neither had a ticket until now.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

- **parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the
  day first, so day and month swap. This is the bug #4471 names.
- **hint.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and then reads the
  date day first. People do what the hint says, so the date swaps. The parser fix does not touch
  this form.
- **one-day move.** The date moves by one day, not by a swap: Tuesday becomes Monday, Lisbon and
  Toronto see different days, an invite arrives before its booking. This looks like a time-zone
  bug. The desk had already read these reports as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`), but
  the tenant rule filed them under `parser`.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject line alone. All
three problems produce the same subjects ("Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the invite"), so one
ticket took them all. Intake tags do not separate them either: they come from the reporter's
browser, and the cause comes from the tenant's form and locale.

## What #4471 showed

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the desk

- When the parser fix ships, expect reports from booking-v3 forms and one-day moves to keep coming.
  They belong on the tickets above, not on #4471.
- Before you merge on subject, check the tenant's form and whether the date swapped or moved by a day.

